ove it, not necessarily SUGGESTED_PHASES. A non-conforming input (e.g., 'Suggested Phases' with space) would also pass the current test, making the regex fix un-tested.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add a third test: `test_phases_regex_accepts_underscore_form()` that creates a fake research-notes.md with '## SUGGESTED_PHASES' (underscore) and a numbered list immediately under it, then asserts _check_suggested_phases_detail returns True. This explicitly covers the regex fix.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/cli/prd/gates.py:134\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Test fixture appends numbered list at end of document, not under SUGGESTED_PHASES\",\n      \"category\": \"tests\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"tests/cli/prd/test_research_notes_roundtrip.py\",\n      \"line\": 73,\n      \"line_range_end\": 73,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"fake_research_notes += \\\"1. Phase one detail\\\\n\\\"\",\n      \"why\": \"The test comment claims 'the loop appends a \\\"- detail\\\" bullet which already satisfies the list-pattern regex', but then appends '1. Phase one detail' at the end. The _check_suggested_phases_detail function searches for the SUGGESTED_PHASES heading, then scans *after* that heading for list items. If the numbered list is appended after all sections (including AMBIGUITIES_FOR_USER), the regex will find it because it scans `content[phases_match.end():]`. This works, but the test structure is fragile — if a future section is added after SUGGESTED_PHASES, the test may pass for the wrong reason.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Insert the numbered list directly under the SUGGESTED_PHASES section in the loop: `if section == \\\"SUGGESTED_PHASES\\\": fake_research_notes += \\\"1. Phase one detail\\\\n\\\"`. This makes the test's structure match the actual document structure and reduces fragility.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/cli/prd/gates.py: